          <div class="partner-body">
            <p class="tag">Towing Gear</p>
            <h2> Aluminum RV Towing Chain Organizer</h2>
            <p><a href="rv-picks.html#towing-chain-organizer">Link to this pick</a></p>
            <p>7 Way Trailer Plug &amp; Chain Holder
              Keeps 7-way Plug and Chains Organized,Fit 2" & 2-1/4" Tongue Jack,Heavy Duty Anti-rust Travel Trailer
              Accessories,Wider Hooks
            </p>
            <aside aria-labelledby="towing-chain-organizer-take" class="pick-assessment">
              <h3 id="towing-chain-organizer-take">Jerry&#x2019;s take</h3>
              <p>I did not buy this item. One came on my new RV. It never occurred to me that an item like this
                was not standard equipment on all RVs.  That is until a few weeks ago when a rookie camper the next site over pulled up with
                a fancy new top-of-the-line RV. I was explaining a few things to him on my RV and pointed
                out the importance of getting the chains and 7-way plug organized and off the ground immediately after
                disconnecting from the tow vehicle to prevent the chains getting tangled or the 7-way cable from getting
                caught, cut, and shorted out under the tongue jack foot (Don't ask me to discuss how I know about this so
                well....).
                We walked over to his new rig, and it wasn't there.</p>
                <p>I strongly recommend getting one of these
                chain / 7-way cable caddies. I'm referencing one that bolts onto the tongue frame like
                mine does. But that will require some serious steel drilling. Amazon actually has
                additional options for caddies that mount to the tongue jack column. But either way, if your bumper pull RV
                didn't come with one, it's an inexpensive way to protect your 7-way cable.</p>
            </aside>

